# CHAPTER 8 · VISUAL BLOCK + NUMBER TRICKS

Column edits — the thing people install multiple-cursor plugins for —
have been native since the 1990s. Plus: vim can do arithmetic on your
text.

## 8.1 VISUAL BLOCK BASICS

`<C-v>`{normal} starts a rectangular selection. Extend it with any
motion. Then:

- `I`{normal} insert at the LEFT edge of every line of the block
- `A`{normal} append at the RIGHT edge of every line
- `d`{normal} / `c`{normal} / `r`{normal} delete/change/replace the
  whole rectangle
- `o`{normal} jumps the cursor to the opposite corner mid-selection
- `gv`{normal} reselects the last selection afterwards

Type the text after `I`{normal}/`A`{normal}, press `<Esc>`{normal},
and it materialises on every line of the block at once.

EXERCISE 1: Comment out all three lines below at once: cursor on the
x of the first line, `<C-v>`{normal} `jj`{normal} to cover the
column, `I`{normal}, type the comment prefix -- and a space, then
`<Esc>`{normal}:

x_out <= a_in and b_in;
y_out <= a_in or b_in;
z_out <= a_in xor b_in;

EXERCISE 2: Lines of DIFFERENT length: `$`{normal} inside a block
selection makes it ragged-right, and `A`{normal} appends at each
line's own end. Select the three lines below with `<C-v>`{normal}
`jj`{normal} `$`{normal}, then `A`{normal}, type a space and [ok],
`<Esc>`{normal}:

latency 12ms
throughput 9000pps
jitter 3ms

## 8.2 INCREMENT AND DECREMENT: C-a C-x

`<C-a>`{normal} increments the first number on or after the cursor;
`<C-x>`{normal} decrements. Counts multiply: `5<C-a>`{normal} adds 5.
Works on decimals, hex (0x1f), even binary — see
[nrformats](nrformats).

EXERCISE 3: Put the cursor anywhere before the 7 on the line below
and press `5<C-a>`{normal}:

retry_count = 7

## 8.3 THE SEQUENCE TRICK: g C-a

In a visual selection, `g<C-a>`{normal} increments each selected line
by an INCREASING amount: first line +1, second +2, third +3...
Select identical copies and it manufactures a numbered sequence.

EXERCISE 4: Leave the first channel line alone. Select the lower
three with `V`{normal} `2j`{normal} (linewise is fine), then press
`g<C-a>`{normal} — they become channel_1, channel_2, channel_3:

channel_0
channel_0
channel_0
channel_0

This is absurdly useful for VHDL port lists, test IDs, array
initialisers — duplicate a line N times with `yy`{normal}
`3p`{normal}, select, `g<C-a>`{normal}, done.

## 8.4 WHEN BLOCK BEATS MACRO (AND VICE VERSA)

Same column, same text on every line → visual block, instantly.
Different positions per line, needs motions to find them → macro.
Both replace 90% of what multiple-cursor plugins are used for, and
they compose with everything else you know.

## WORKOUT

Build this from scratch in 30 seconds, starting from one line
reading data(0) <= x"00";  — `yy`{normal} `7p`{normal}, block-select
the indices, `g<C-a>`{normal}. Eight numbered assignment lines, two
commands.

Key reading: [visual-block](visual-block), [v_b_I](v_b_I),
[v_b_A](v_b_A), [CTRL-A](CTRL-A), [v_g_CTRL-A](v_g_CTRL-A)

